    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. one more thing—we've had some issues with stuff getting contaminated or spoiling faster than it should even when we follow the temps. are there any specific \"invisible\" or sneaky ways these bacteria like C. perfringens can get into the food or thrive even if the kitchen looks clean?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "C. perfringens can proliferate in food that exceeds the safe temperature zone (40°C to 60°C) for extended periods, which can happen if food is left out at room temperature. Cross-contamination can also occur if kitchen surfaces, equipment, or utensils that came into contact with raw poultry are not properly sanitized before use with cooked poultry. Additionally, employees handling the food and failing to make contact with safe surfaces, such as washing hands before and after handling food, can be sources of unseen contamination. Keeping sanitary practices stringent, achieving rapid cooling, and ensuring low-temperature storage are key to preventing bacterial growth.",
      "source": "target",
